WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.994
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.782 --> 00:00:06.490
SEAN GINEVAN: Hi.

00:00:06.490 --> 00:00:07.448
My name's Sean Ginevan.

00:00:07.448 --> 00:00:12.310
I work on a team here at Google
called Android Enterprise.

00:00:12.310 --> 00:00:16.420
Our team is responsible for the
business-to-business success

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:17.570
of the Android platform.

00:00:17.570 --> 00:00:21.460
So if you're an IT
organization like an HSBC

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:26.470
or a Walmart or a
SAP, you're often

00:00:26.470 --> 00:00:29.110
interacting with
folks on our team

00:00:29.110 --> 00:00:31.510
who are working
with partners that

00:00:31.510 --> 00:00:33.790
help you deploy those
devices, that help you build

00:00:33.790 --> 00:00:36.100
applications for those devices.

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:40.270
Jon and myself work with
large enterprise ISVs as well,

00:00:40.270 --> 00:00:43.230
folks like Salesforce,
SAP, Manhattan Associates,

00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:45.562
ServiceNow, folks like that.

00:00:45.562 --> 00:00:47.020
But we find ourselves
being brought

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:48.410
into a lot of conversations.

00:00:48.410 --> 00:00:49.960
It feels like that
right now we're

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:51.460
at this interesting
transition point

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:55.310
where IT is being asked
to do more brand things.

00:00:55.310 --> 00:00:58.000
And as an example,
Jon and I were

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:02.660
in a conversation with a
large retailer, and they said,

00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:05.379
hey, we really want
to go and optimize

00:01:05.379 --> 00:01:07.330
our consumer experience.

00:01:07.330 --> 00:01:10.433
And I told them, well
hey, you should probably

00:01:10.433 --> 00:01:12.100
be thinking about
building for the web--

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:15.175
for a lot of different reasons
that we'll talk about today.

00:01:15.175 --> 00:01:17.050
And then they said,
fantastic, and then we'll

00:01:17.050 --> 00:01:18.758
use that for all of
our store associates.

00:01:18.758 --> 00:01:19.970
I said, whoa, whoa, whoa.

00:01:19.970 --> 00:01:20.620
Wait, wait.

00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:22.090
Not so fast.

00:01:22.090 --> 00:01:25.420
Let's talk about what you want
to do for this project, what

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:28.628
that means for running mobile
devices in the warehouse,

00:01:28.628 --> 00:01:30.420
and figure out which
technologies are going

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:31.930
to be appropriate for you.

00:01:31.930 --> 00:01:35.570
And that's what we want to get
out of today's presentation.

00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:38.290
So let's talk a little bit
about an overview of what

00:01:38.290 --> 00:01:40.270
we want to get out of
today's presentation.

00:01:40.270 --> 00:01:42.640
What we really are
going to talk about

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:44.440
is an overview of
web technologies

00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:46.150
as they apply
specifically to Android.

00:01:46.150 --> 00:01:48.490
We'll touch a little bit
about other mobile platforms

00:01:48.490 --> 00:01:50.570
on the market as well.

00:01:50.570 --> 00:01:54.910
We'll also talk about how
to think about building app

00:01:54.910 --> 00:01:55.730
experiences.

00:01:55.730 --> 00:01:58.810
So what are some of the design
and development considerations

00:01:58.810 --> 00:02:02.560
you want to think about when
building out web experiences

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:04.300
specifically for mobile?

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:07.480
And we'll talk about some
considerations on trade-offs

00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:09.970
as you think about, on a
project-by-project basis,

00:02:09.970 --> 00:02:13.030
should I be building for the
web or building for native?

00:02:13.030 --> 00:02:15.400
This is not designed to
be a deep presentation

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:16.670
about building for the web.

00:02:16.670 --> 00:02:19.270
It's not designed to be
a presentation on how

00:02:19.270 --> 00:02:22.240
to implement service
workers, provide code level

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:25.510
implementation for
PWA or for native.

00:02:25.510 --> 00:02:28.280
We're also going to not
cover things like gray areas.

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:30.430
There's a lot of
initiatives around,

00:02:30.430 --> 00:02:32.380
how do I make apps
more discoverable?

00:02:32.380 --> 00:02:34.690
How do I make them
surface and search, things

00:02:34.690 --> 00:02:35.943
like Instant Apps on play.

00:02:35.943 --> 00:02:37.360
We're not going
to talk about that

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:39.370
as well because I
only have 50 minutes

00:02:39.370 --> 00:02:41.290
to get through the content.

00:02:41.290 --> 00:02:43.110
Thank you for
fixing the clicker.

00:02:43.110 --> 00:02:48.580
So we'll start to frame out
this morning's presentation

00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:50.450
with a little bit of a story.

00:02:50.450 --> 00:02:53.440
How many of you here
in the room traveled

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:56.500
from outside of San Francisco
and needed a hotel in order

00:02:56.500 --> 00:02:59.020
to be able to attend Next?

00:02:59.020 --> 00:03:01.962
So most of you, about 2/3.

00:03:01.962 --> 00:03:03.670
So when you went
through that experience,

00:03:03.670 --> 00:03:06.460
the likelihood-- this is
data from a talk McKinsey

00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:08.680
did back in 2018.

00:03:08.680 --> 00:03:13.270
They looked at some data around
how people in the travel sector

00:03:13.270 --> 00:03:16.480
were actually looking through
their purchase journeys.

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:19.900
And what they found was
that's 63% of those journeys

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:23.320
so from, hey, I'm thinking
about traveling to Aruba,

00:03:23.320 --> 00:03:26.350
to actually getting my
plane tickets purchased,

00:03:26.350 --> 00:03:30.830
came from a mix of both mobile
and cross-device journeys.

00:03:30.830 --> 00:03:35.770
So what that meant is I likely
am on the train on my phone

00:03:35.770 --> 00:03:38.980
going, gosh, it would be
really great to go to Aruba.

00:03:38.980 --> 00:03:42.860
And then maybe I go through,
and I do some research.

00:03:42.860 --> 00:03:45.297
I pick that session
up on my desktop.

00:03:45.297 --> 00:03:46.630
I do a little bit more research.

00:03:46.630 --> 00:03:49.102
I start to narrow
down the hotels.

00:03:49.102 --> 00:03:51.310
Google doesn't pay me enough,
so the five-star hotels

00:03:51.310 --> 00:03:53.260
get thrown out immediately.

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:56.590
And I go back and forth until
I eventually go and make

00:03:56.590 --> 00:03:58.550
a purchasing decision.

00:03:58.550 --> 00:04:01.850
What they also found was
that 31% of those journeys

00:04:01.850 --> 00:04:03.200
came through search.

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:06.477
So it's not that I went directly
to travel.com or whatever

00:04:06.477 --> 00:04:07.310
website you choose--

00:04:07.310 --> 00:04:09.650
Google Flights, as an
example, or Google Maps

00:04:09.650 --> 00:04:11.510
to go buy a hotel--

00:04:11.510 --> 00:04:14.720
I went and used search to go
through and look for things

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:16.760
like cheap flights to Aruba.

00:04:16.760 --> 00:04:18.800
Which hotels are best in Aruba?

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:25.430
And I used that as a catalyst
to starting my purchase journey.

00:04:25.430 --> 00:04:29.100
26% of those purchases
came from the mobile web.

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:33.330
So that meant that somebody
in this cross-device journey

00:04:33.330 --> 00:04:36.150
was going through and
finishing that actual purchase

00:04:36.150 --> 00:04:38.020
on their phone.

00:04:38.020 --> 00:04:41.000
And this is really important
because it means that we really

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:43.220
need to be optimizing
our experiences

00:04:43.220 --> 00:04:44.660
for a mix of different devices.

00:04:44.660 --> 00:04:48.330
We don't necessarily know,
from the brand perspective,

00:04:48.330 --> 00:04:50.330
which particular
modality a person's

00:04:50.330 --> 00:04:53.540
going to be using when
they actually go and enter

00:04:53.540 --> 00:04:54.290
into my site.

00:04:54.290 --> 00:04:58.280
And that is also likely
true for employee apps.

00:04:58.280 --> 00:05:00.650
We're seeing a lot
of employees use

00:05:00.650 --> 00:05:03.110
a mix of mobile
devices and laptops

00:05:03.110 --> 00:05:04.650
to actually get their work done.

00:05:04.650 --> 00:05:06.260
And so it's really
important, as we

00:05:06.260 --> 00:05:09.140
think about what tools are we
enabling our employees with,

00:05:09.140 --> 00:05:11.970
that we be enabling for both.

00:05:11.970 --> 00:05:14.280
Now, the web has changed a lot.

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:16.590
Web pages used to
be super static.

00:05:16.590 --> 00:05:18.900
They used to load
everything at once.

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:20.850
And for whatever reason,
back in the '90s--

00:05:20.850 --> 00:05:23.110
I think mainly as developers
were like, oh, my gosh.

00:05:23.110 --> 00:05:25.410
If I add one more thing,
it'll be that much better.

00:05:25.410 --> 00:05:27.840
Look, there's a blink
tag, and I figured out

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:29.020
how animations work.

00:05:29.020 --> 00:05:31.830
So let's make things scroll
for inexplicable reasons--

00:05:31.830 --> 00:05:33.155
and overloaded the site.

00:05:33.155 --> 00:05:35.850
So this is actually an example
from Stack Overflow that they

00:05:35.850 --> 00:05:37.530
did for April Fools'.

00:05:37.530 --> 00:05:42.210
They actually redesigned
the entire site circa 1990.

00:05:42.210 --> 00:05:45.000
So the good news is that,
at least on the desktop web,

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:46.290
things have changed a lot.

00:05:46.290 --> 00:05:49.500
We're making sites
much more interactive.

00:05:49.500 --> 00:05:53.415
You can actually now run
Windows 2000 in a browser.

00:05:53.415 --> 00:05:56.040
I don't know why you'd do that,
but it's a really great example

00:05:56.040 --> 00:05:58.850
of WebAssembly.

00:05:58.850 --> 00:06:01.610
The challenge is that the
mobile web has traditionally

00:06:01.610 --> 00:06:03.110
been lackluster.

00:06:03.110 --> 00:06:05.510
A lot of brands,
many organizations,

00:06:05.510 --> 00:06:08.240
use the mobile web
as a place-holder,

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:10.010
a static place-holder
to advertise

00:06:10.010 --> 00:06:12.830
the mobile app they've
built. And basically, there's

00:06:12.830 --> 00:06:15.530
a link that says, visit
us on the App Store

00:06:15.530 --> 00:06:17.208
or visit us on Google Play.

00:06:17.208 --> 00:06:19.500
Or sometimes they're not
particularly good at this game

00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:21.333
so they just they visit
us on the App Store,

00:06:21.333 --> 00:06:23.773
and it really infuriates
us Android users.

00:06:23.773 --> 00:06:25.190
And the challenge
there is that we

00:06:25.190 --> 00:06:27.620
know these patterns of
development behavior

00:06:27.620 --> 00:06:30.160
don't work.

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:32.600
The Google+ team, before
we wound down Google+,

00:06:32.600 --> 00:06:37.400
actually did an experiment,
and they added an advertisement

00:06:37.400 --> 00:06:41.440
to the Google+ app,
to plus.Google.com.

00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:44.408
So when you went on your mobile
phone to plus.Google.com,

00:06:44.408 --> 00:06:46.700
you saw this ad that's up
here on the screen that says,

00:06:46.700 --> 00:06:47.840
get the app.

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:50.690
And what they found in that
survey was that 69% of users

00:06:50.690 --> 00:06:52.940
abandoned the site entirely.

00:06:52.940 --> 00:06:54.800
They neither went to
the mobile website,

00:06:54.800 --> 00:06:58.460
nor did they actually go through
and download the application.

00:06:58.460 --> 00:07:00.590
Say what you will about
Google+ as a platform.

00:07:00.590 --> 00:07:03.860
I think this is indicative
of a broader user behavior.

00:07:03.860 --> 00:07:07.080
The activation energy to
download an application,

00:07:07.080 --> 00:07:09.530
particularly as a consumer
who's not really attached

00:07:09.530 --> 00:07:11.300
to your brand, is high.

00:07:11.300 --> 00:07:12.220
I have to go to Play.

00:07:12.220 --> 00:07:13.220
I have to click Install.

00:07:13.220 --> 00:07:14.060
I have to wait.

00:07:14.060 --> 00:07:16.370
Then I have to sign
up for an account.

00:07:16.370 --> 00:07:19.640
And if I've never
heard of your brand

00:07:19.640 --> 00:07:23.280
before, why am I going to
go through all of that work?

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:25.520
And so it's really
important, I believe,

00:07:25.520 --> 00:07:30.380
to be thinking about at least
having much of my experience

00:07:30.380 --> 00:07:31.760
optimized for the mobile web.

00:07:31.760 --> 00:07:33.500
It doesn't necessarily
mean abandon

00:07:33.500 --> 00:07:34.970
your mobile application.

00:07:34.970 --> 00:07:38.270
Spotify has actually gone
through this journey where

00:07:38.270 --> 00:07:42.260
they've said, hey, we want to
allow users to discover music.

00:07:42.260 --> 00:07:45.170
So if anybody has been
to BottleRock Napa

00:07:45.170 --> 00:07:47.510
Valley or other music
festivals, likely you

00:07:47.510 --> 00:07:49.310
get this custom
Spotify playlist that

00:07:49.310 --> 00:07:52.520
says it's designed to get
you excited about the bands

00:07:52.520 --> 00:07:53.640
you're going to go see.

00:07:53.640 --> 00:07:56.060
And it used to be you'd click
that link and it says, hi.

00:07:56.060 --> 00:07:57.500
Go download the Spotify app.

00:07:57.500 --> 00:07:59.880
And if your answer
is, what's Spotify,

00:07:59.880 --> 00:08:02.160
you probably just abandon
that experience entirely.

00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:05.020
And so they've really seen
a high degree of conversions

00:08:05.020 --> 00:08:09.830
since opening Spotify up to the
web of people who are actually

00:08:09.830 --> 00:08:12.920
finding out about the service
through these other brand

00:08:12.920 --> 00:08:15.470
interactions, like music
festivals, like news

00:08:15.470 --> 00:08:18.670
articles, and the like.

00:08:18.670 --> 00:08:21.340
So we know that building for
the mobile web is important.

00:08:21.340 --> 00:08:23.910
So if we're going to build
for a built better mobile web,

00:08:23.910 --> 00:08:25.952
what are some of the things
that we're absolutely

00:08:25.952 --> 00:08:27.700
going to want to consider?

00:08:27.700 --> 00:08:31.650
One is around making our
web experience as engaging.

00:08:31.650 --> 00:08:35.280
That starts with UX and
really optimizing around

00:08:35.280 --> 00:08:40.600
the variety of form factors and
screens that the web presents.

00:08:40.600 --> 00:08:41.970
Not everything's a phone.

00:08:41.970 --> 00:08:43.439
Not everything's a tablet.

00:08:43.439 --> 00:08:46.380
Foldables are going to make this
even more confusing because it

00:08:46.380 --> 00:08:48.482
will be both in my pocket.

00:08:48.482 --> 00:08:50.940
And so we really want to make
sure that we're making really

00:08:50.940 --> 00:08:53.520
dynamic UX experiences.

00:08:53.520 --> 00:08:55.770
We want to make our sites
engaging through things,

00:08:55.770 --> 00:08:57.395
potentially, like
hardware integration.

00:08:57.395 --> 00:09:00.120
So we'll talk about
hardware in a minute--

00:09:00.120 --> 00:09:02.940
through push notifications,
so being able to go through

00:09:02.940 --> 00:09:05.190
and re-engage our users
that come to our site.

00:09:05.190 --> 00:09:09.900
Hey, I saw you liked this
pair of black slacks.

00:09:09.900 --> 00:09:12.660
They're now 10% off, or
we now have them back

00:09:12.660 --> 00:09:15.010
in stock, which happens to me.

00:09:15.010 --> 00:09:17.820
And then credentials, being able
to go through and actually--

00:09:17.820 --> 00:09:19.818
if the user has
saved credentials,

00:09:19.818 --> 00:09:21.360
being able to reuse
those credentials

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:23.950
or even potentially being
able to single sign-on

00:09:23.950 --> 00:09:27.600
through credential
stores like Google.

00:09:27.600 --> 00:09:30.600
Another consideration is
making the web installable.

00:09:30.600 --> 00:09:34.850
So if I, as a user,
have visited your brand,

00:09:34.850 --> 00:09:37.090
and I'm really showing a
high degree of engagement,

00:09:37.090 --> 00:09:38.382
how do I get them to come back?

00:09:38.382 --> 00:09:41.790
Now, one option may be, hey,
go download my mobile app.

00:09:41.790 --> 00:09:43.710
It really seems
like you like me.

00:09:43.710 --> 00:09:45.210
Another way is just
to say, hey, I'm

00:09:45.210 --> 00:09:47.418
going to add this web
experience to your home screen.

00:09:47.418 --> 00:09:49.230
And this experience
might not be everything

00:09:49.230 --> 00:09:51.610
that you want to do for
your mobile experience,

00:09:51.610 --> 00:09:54.570
but you're bringing that
user into the experience,

00:09:54.570 --> 00:09:56.058
transition them
through, hey, I'm

00:09:56.058 --> 00:09:57.600
going to add this
to the home screen.

00:09:57.600 --> 00:10:00.060
Maybe with even more
engagement, I keep them there,

00:10:00.060 --> 00:10:02.770
or maybe I bring them
onto my mobile app.

00:10:02.770 --> 00:10:04.590
And so, in addition,
for those of you

00:10:04.590 --> 00:10:07.650
that are enterprise developers,
you can take web experiences

00:10:07.650 --> 00:10:10.110
and surface them through
Managed Google Play.

00:10:10.110 --> 00:10:13.230
Managed Google Play is a
version of the Play Store

00:10:13.230 --> 00:10:15.180
that's available
for enterprises who

00:10:15.180 --> 00:10:17.370
want to distribute
internal applications out

00:10:17.370 --> 00:10:18.910
to their employees.

00:10:18.910 --> 00:10:21.440
And so you can create
basically your own private Play

00:10:21.440 --> 00:10:23.390
Store that distributes
native apps,

00:10:23.390 --> 00:10:27.780
and now you can distribute
web apps as well.

00:10:27.780 --> 00:10:30.240
We want to make
the sites reliable.

00:10:30.240 --> 00:10:34.170
For all the talk about 5G,
connectivity is not perfect.

00:10:34.170 --> 00:10:38.250
I read a stat recently
that roughly half

00:10:38.250 --> 00:10:41.820
of all connections on
the web are still coming

00:10:41.820 --> 00:10:45.070
from 3G devices or 3G networks.

00:10:45.070 --> 00:10:47.690
So we really need to be
thinking about performance

00:10:47.690 --> 00:10:50.190
optimization-- which I guess
falls more in the fast bucket--

00:10:50.190 --> 00:10:54.300
but also, hey, I'm going
from London to Paris,

00:10:54.300 --> 00:10:56.150
and I go through a tunnel.

00:10:56.150 --> 00:10:58.430
I get back online, and I
go through a tunnel again.

00:10:58.430 --> 00:11:00.210
And I go back online, and
I'm on a tunnel again.

00:11:00.210 --> 00:11:01.668
This happened to
Jon and I recently

00:11:01.668 --> 00:11:03.990
because we were both in
France visiting customers.

00:11:03.990 --> 00:11:06.750
And I was not particularly
productive on those websites

00:11:06.750 --> 00:11:10.010
that didn't know how to
optimize for offline.

00:11:10.010 --> 00:11:11.330
And then, finally, fast.

00:11:11.330 --> 00:11:13.830
So we really want to make sure
we're optimizing performance.

00:11:13.830 --> 00:11:17.750
We know that low bandwidth, high
latency connections are still

00:11:17.750 --> 00:11:19.880
out there, and we want
to make sure that we're

00:11:19.880 --> 00:11:20.870
optimizing for those.

00:11:20.870 --> 00:11:23.900
And we'll talk about
some of that in a minute.

00:11:23.900 --> 00:11:26.380
One way to enable these
reliable experiences

00:11:26.380 --> 00:11:27.980
is through service workers.

00:11:27.980 --> 00:11:29.980
If you think about the
old way of doing the web,

00:11:29.980 --> 00:11:33.217
the old way is I would basically
have a server out there

00:11:33.217 --> 00:11:34.300
somewhere on the internet.

00:11:34.300 --> 00:11:36.160
My browser would go and
pop up, and it would

00:11:36.160 --> 00:11:39.490
download everything at once.

00:11:39.490 --> 00:11:40.600
That was fine.

00:11:40.600 --> 00:11:43.480
But as sites became more
complex and more interactive,

00:11:43.480 --> 00:11:47.680
it became really, really tricky
to go through and do everything

00:11:47.680 --> 00:11:48.470
at once.

00:11:48.470 --> 00:11:50.320
And so the idea
of service workers

00:11:50.320 --> 00:11:53.830
is that you can build out a
client site proxy in JavaScript

00:11:53.830 --> 00:11:56.433
and have a little agent
inside the browser that's

00:11:56.433 --> 00:11:57.850
separate from the
DOM and separate

00:11:57.850 --> 00:12:01.150
from the main thread that
can do work on your behalf.

00:12:01.150 --> 00:12:03.520
Some of that work are things
like notification APIs

00:12:03.520 --> 00:12:05.800
that we'll talk
about in a minute,

00:12:05.800 --> 00:12:09.280
push APIs, which allow you to
go and get push messages to go

00:12:09.280 --> 00:12:12.310
and do other things,
background synchronization,

00:12:12.310 --> 00:12:15.730
so you can go through and
start to go and paint the page

00:12:15.730 --> 00:12:18.958
and load additional
things in the background

00:12:18.958 --> 00:12:21.250
as the user starts to figure
out where they want to go.

00:12:23.830 --> 00:12:25.943
When we think about
the web, though, it's

00:12:25.943 --> 00:12:27.610
great to have a bunch
of technical tools

00:12:27.610 --> 00:12:29.483
that are at our disposal.

00:12:29.483 --> 00:12:31.650
But one thing that I often
coach people about is you

00:12:31.650 --> 00:12:34.990
really want to build out
your UX to be dynamic.

00:12:34.990 --> 00:12:38.260
One way to do that is to use
a lot of the design patterns

00:12:38.260 --> 00:12:40.870
that are already in material.

00:12:40.870 --> 00:12:43.000
You can use things
like responsive grids

00:12:43.000 --> 00:12:46.770
and breakpoints to change the
UI as the screen size changes.

00:12:46.770 --> 00:12:50.710
Twitter, with Twitter Lite, has
done an excellent job of this

00:12:50.710 --> 00:12:53.540
where, as you go and
expand the screen,

00:12:53.540 --> 00:12:55.930
not only am I changing the
horizontal width of what's

00:12:55.930 --> 00:12:59.530
being displayed, but as I
go from smartphone to tablet

00:12:59.530 --> 00:13:02.230
and out to desktop,
I'm actually uncovering

00:13:02.230 --> 00:13:06.370
additional functionality
and additional content

00:13:06.370 --> 00:13:08.410
because I now have
the additional screen

00:13:08.410 --> 00:13:10.010
real estate to do so.

00:13:10.010 --> 00:13:14.110
So you can see on
the right-hand side

00:13:14.110 --> 00:13:18.610
as the browser pane
goes and expands,

00:13:18.610 --> 00:13:20.890
Twitter actually goes
and says, hey, here's

00:13:20.890 --> 00:13:21.890
who you should follow.

00:13:21.890 --> 00:13:25.420
Here's trends in San Francisco,
in addition to the Twitter feed

00:13:25.420 --> 00:13:27.820
for Google Next 19.

00:13:27.820 --> 00:13:30.820
These design
patterns are not only

00:13:30.820 --> 00:13:32.200
relevant for the mobile web.

00:13:32.200 --> 00:13:33.850
These are also
design patterns are

00:13:33.850 --> 00:13:36.380
highly relevant for
native apps as well.

00:13:36.380 --> 00:13:38.260
Many of you are aware
that Android native

00:13:38.260 --> 00:13:40.360
apps can run on Chromebooks.

00:13:40.360 --> 00:13:43.390
And certainly Chromebooks--
if you're designing

00:13:43.390 --> 00:13:44.830
for Chromebooks--

00:13:44.830 --> 00:13:47.080
need to be more
responsive, things

00:13:47.080 --> 00:13:49.990
like window resizing,
maximizing, and whatnot.

00:13:49.990 --> 00:13:52.570
It's not particularly awesome
to be on your Chromebook

00:13:52.570 --> 00:13:53.710
and go, cool.

00:13:53.710 --> 00:13:54.430
A phone app.

00:13:54.430 --> 00:13:57.460
And it just looks
exactly like a phone app.

00:13:57.460 --> 00:14:00.670
You want to be
designing for both.

00:14:00.670 --> 00:14:03.693
One final thing about the web is
you can also design those apps

00:14:03.693 --> 00:14:05.110
and run those apps
in full screen.

00:14:05.110 --> 00:14:08.440
So you can actually have
your applications, your web

00:14:08.440 --> 00:14:11.110
applications, run almost as if
they were like a native app.

00:14:13.680 --> 00:14:15.650
Another way to think
about dynamic workflows

00:14:15.650 --> 00:14:17.000
is through hardware integration.

00:14:17.000 --> 00:14:19.580
So obviously,
cellular and Wi-Fi,

00:14:19.580 --> 00:14:22.850
those are going to be your base
connectivity mechanisms that

00:14:22.850 --> 00:14:26.540
are allowing you to go and
get content into the browser.

00:14:26.540 --> 00:14:28.550
But there's a lot of other
hardware capabilities

00:14:28.550 --> 00:14:32.150
that come with building out
for PWA, things like GPS.

00:14:32.150 --> 00:14:33.740
Hey, I want to go
through and find

00:14:33.740 --> 00:14:36.148
what store locations
are near me or where

00:14:36.148 --> 00:14:37.190
something is in the mall.

00:14:37.190 --> 00:14:40.340
I can pop up my location and
provide some interactivity back

00:14:40.340 --> 00:14:41.240
to the user.

00:14:41.240 --> 00:14:44.180
Or potentially even
things like the camera

00:14:44.180 --> 00:14:47.750
for object recognition or
microphone for voice search.

00:14:47.750 --> 00:14:50.902
You have a lot of these hardware
capabilities at your disposal,

00:14:50.902 --> 00:14:53.360
whether you're building for
the web or building for native.

00:14:53.360 --> 00:14:56.120
Jon will talk about in a
minute that this is not,

00:14:56.120 --> 00:14:57.680
obviously, the
totality of hardware

00:14:57.680 --> 00:14:58.885
that's available on Android.

00:14:58.885 --> 00:15:00.260
And so you'll need
to be thinking

00:15:00.260 --> 00:15:02.030
about the level of
hardware interactivity

00:15:02.030 --> 00:15:04.640
you have when deciding
which tool set you want

00:15:04.640 --> 00:15:07.900
to build for your projects.

00:15:07.900 --> 00:15:09.910
Push notifications
are a really great way

00:15:09.910 --> 00:15:12.610
to actually engage
with your users

00:15:12.610 --> 00:15:16.380
and actually get them to
re-engage with your website.

00:15:16.380 --> 00:15:19.270
One of the pieces of
guidance I give is, please

00:15:19.270 --> 00:15:21.730
don't as soon as I hit
the site and say, hi, I'd

00:15:21.730 --> 00:15:23.980
like to subscribe you
to push notifications,

00:15:23.980 --> 00:15:28.360
because one, that's
obnoxious, and two,

00:15:28.360 --> 00:15:31.390
I have not really shown a ton
of brand loyalty to you yet.

00:15:31.390 --> 00:15:33.520
You haven't necessarily
earned the right

00:15:33.520 --> 00:15:35.350
to go through and
start sending me

00:15:35.350 --> 00:15:37.150
a bunch of additional
notifications.

00:15:37.150 --> 00:15:39.550
But if I'm-- and Lancome
has actually done this

00:15:39.550 --> 00:15:40.510
on their site--

00:15:40.510 --> 00:15:42.550
shown a high degree
of interest on things,

00:15:42.550 --> 00:15:44.350
I'm actually navigating
through the site,

00:15:44.350 --> 00:15:48.370
I'm engaging with a lot
of different content,

00:15:48.370 --> 00:15:50.710
I can go and not only
ask for push notification

00:15:50.710 --> 00:15:52.780
but, more importantly,
emphasize what

00:15:52.780 --> 00:15:55.510
I'm going to get from
subscribing to those push

00:15:55.510 --> 00:15:57.110
notifications.

00:15:57.110 --> 00:15:59.110
So as an example,
I can emphasize--

00:15:59.110 --> 00:16:02.755
in the case of Lancome that,
hey, let me tell you what

00:16:02.755 --> 00:16:03.880
some of the best deals are.

00:16:03.880 --> 00:16:05.297
Let me tell you
about new products

00:16:05.297 --> 00:16:07.000
we're going to bring
out for the season,

00:16:07.000 --> 00:16:10.190
and I'll bring that
content to you right away.

00:16:10.190 --> 00:16:11.940
One of the things about
push notifications

00:16:11.940 --> 00:16:14.250
is, in Android,
when you subscribe

00:16:14.250 --> 00:16:17.040
to push notifications,
Android will wake you up

00:16:17.040 --> 00:16:20.360
in the background and actually
surface that push notification.

00:16:20.360 --> 00:16:22.572
In desktop, the browser
actually needs to be open

00:16:22.572 --> 00:16:24.030
or the browser
needs to be resonant

00:16:24.030 --> 00:16:26.010
in memory for those push
notifications to work.

00:16:26.010 --> 00:16:29.347
So the reliability of
those push notifications

00:16:29.347 --> 00:16:30.930
may change depending
on whether or not

00:16:30.930 --> 00:16:35.330
you're designing on
desktop versus web.

00:16:35.330 --> 00:16:38.870
Credential Management also comes
up quite a bit from customers.

00:16:38.870 --> 00:16:41.090
And the good news is
that there's really

00:16:41.090 --> 00:16:43.095
great APIs for the
web to handle things

00:16:43.095 --> 00:16:44.220
like Credential Management.

00:16:44.220 --> 00:16:48.943
So you can actually go
through and say, hey, surface

00:16:48.943 --> 00:16:49.610
out to the user.

00:16:49.610 --> 00:16:50.985
I know you're
signing in locally.

00:16:50.985 --> 00:16:52.700
You're not clicking
in sign with Google.

00:16:52.700 --> 00:16:55.520
You're actually using local
account credentials to Acme,

00:16:55.520 --> 00:16:57.890
Inc. Do you want to
save these credentials?

00:16:57.890 --> 00:16:59.390
And Credential
Management allows you

00:16:59.390 --> 00:17:01.170
to save those into the browser.

00:17:01.170 --> 00:17:03.740
And in cases like
Chrome, you can actually

00:17:03.740 --> 00:17:06.670
save those credentials
across devices.

00:17:06.670 --> 00:17:11.450
So I can hop between my
Android device, my Chromebook,

00:17:11.450 --> 00:17:12.661
my Windows desktop.

00:17:12.661 --> 00:17:14.119
Why I would use
that, I don't know.

00:17:14.119 --> 00:17:17.030
But I can go through and
have those credentials

00:17:17.030 --> 00:17:20.089
be stored across devices
not just across sessions.

00:17:20.089 --> 00:17:22.310
And you can decide
whether or not

00:17:22.310 --> 00:17:26.119
you want to store the entire
set of credentials, so username

00:17:26.119 --> 00:17:28.460
and password, or just
auto-fill username

00:17:28.460 --> 00:17:30.120
and have the user
type in password.

00:17:30.120 --> 00:17:31.370
Those decisions are up to you.

00:17:35.170 --> 00:17:37.430
The next is to create an
installable experience.

00:17:37.430 --> 00:17:39.560
Starbucks has done this
with their native--

00:17:39.560 --> 00:17:40.060
sorry.

00:17:40.060 --> 00:17:40.810
With their native.

00:17:40.810 --> 00:17:43.870
With their web-based
application for doing things

00:17:43.870 --> 00:17:47.920
like checking in Starbucks
rewards or going through

00:17:47.920 --> 00:17:50.890
and ordering lattes from
the local Starbucks.

00:17:50.890 --> 00:17:54.190
What the Add to Home Screen
prompt allows me to do

00:17:54.190 --> 00:17:58.840
is do just that, add that PWA
experience to my home screen.

00:17:58.840 --> 00:18:01.330
That allows the
web page to install

00:18:01.330 --> 00:18:04.450
much like a native
app and with things

00:18:04.450 --> 00:18:10.510
like an app menu in the apps
settings tray in Android.

00:18:10.510 --> 00:18:13.570
It adds, obviously, home
screen icons and the like.

00:18:13.570 --> 00:18:15.580
But again, you really
want to be triggering

00:18:15.580 --> 00:18:18.460
this prompt at a meaningful
moment for the user.

00:18:18.460 --> 00:18:21.520
I don't necessarily want
to install the Starbucks

00:18:21.520 --> 00:18:24.850
experience the first time
I go to Starbucks.com.

00:18:24.850 --> 00:18:28.990
I might want to install the
Starbucks experience when I've

00:18:28.990 --> 00:18:31.030
successfully ordered
something or I'm

00:18:31.030 --> 00:18:33.520
checking my account or at
least I've logged in and saved

00:18:33.520 --> 00:18:35.870
credentials, because
the likelihood that I'll

00:18:35.870 --> 00:18:38.840
want to come back to
that is much higher.

00:18:38.840 --> 00:18:42.630
I mentioned Managed Google Play.

00:18:42.630 --> 00:18:45.510
You can also surface
PWAs for your brand

00:18:45.510 --> 00:18:46.810
into Google Play itself.

00:18:46.810 --> 00:18:49.200
So if you're
entirely on the web,

00:18:49.200 --> 00:18:51.960
you can actually publish
these web experiences

00:18:51.960 --> 00:18:54.570
both for employees as well as
for the consumer Play Store.

00:18:58.430 --> 00:19:00.980
When you're distributing these
applications through Managed

00:19:00.980 --> 00:19:05.990
Play, you can decide both who
you want these applications

00:19:05.990 --> 00:19:08.330
to go to in your install base.

00:19:08.330 --> 00:19:11.150
So you can decide, hey, this
is a financial workflow app.

00:19:11.150 --> 00:19:13.130
Don't publish it to
the entire company.

00:19:13.130 --> 00:19:16.310
Only publish it out to
the people in finance.

00:19:16.310 --> 00:19:19.190
And you don't have to be a
G Suite customer to do that.

00:19:19.190 --> 00:19:22.430
There are tools out there where
you can federate Managed Google

00:19:22.430 --> 00:19:26.300
Play to your existing Active
Directory or other LDAP data

00:19:26.300 --> 00:19:30.680
store so you don't have to go
and create a G Suite domain

00:19:30.680 --> 00:19:32.660
or create Gmail
accounts for every user

00:19:32.660 --> 00:19:34.860
inside of your organization.

00:19:34.860 --> 00:19:38.220
And as you go and create the App
Store listings for your users,

00:19:38.220 --> 00:19:40.580
you can decide how you want
that application to render.

00:19:40.580 --> 00:19:42.800
Do you want it to
render like a website

00:19:42.800 --> 00:19:46.070
and so show things like the
toolbar and the address bar?

00:19:46.070 --> 00:19:48.495
Do you want it to be more
of a minimal experience

00:19:48.495 --> 00:19:50.120
so users can
differentiate when they're

00:19:50.120 --> 00:19:52.430
in a native app versus
when they're in the web,

00:19:52.430 --> 00:19:57.980
or do you want to just have
it be a full-screen experience

00:19:57.980 --> 00:20:01.070
and feel much more like
a native application?

00:20:01.070 --> 00:20:03.260
You can publish these
applications out

00:20:03.260 --> 00:20:05.210
by a few different mechanisms.

00:20:05.210 --> 00:20:07.480
Certainly, you can publish
to the Play Console.

00:20:07.480 --> 00:20:10.430
But a couple of new mechanisms
we've recently introduced

00:20:10.430 --> 00:20:13.250
are custom publishing APIs,
which allow you to basically

00:20:13.250 --> 00:20:15.350
script a lot of
application management

00:20:15.350 --> 00:20:18.580
to Managed Play as well as
the Managed Play iframes.

00:20:18.580 --> 00:20:21.180
So for those of you that have
a mobility management system,

00:20:21.180 --> 00:20:24.200
you can now control Play
directly from your EMM.

00:20:27.130 --> 00:20:30.270
Who here has seen
this screen before?

00:20:30.270 --> 00:20:32.177
Now, I love when I
go offline because it

00:20:32.177 --> 00:20:34.010
means I get to go and
play the dinosaur game

00:20:34.010 --> 00:20:38.600
and figure out whether or
not I can beat my high score.

00:20:38.600 --> 00:20:41.310
And from this animation, I'm
not particularly good at it.

00:20:41.310 --> 00:20:44.605
But the point here is is
that as much as I might love

00:20:44.605 --> 00:20:45.980
this level of
interactivity, it's

00:20:45.980 --> 00:20:48.140
not necessarily great for
your brand experience.

00:20:48.140 --> 00:20:50.972
It's not going to help me
continue to research hotels.

00:20:50.972 --> 00:20:53.180
It's not going to help me
figure out whether or not I

00:20:53.180 --> 00:20:54.690
want to go purchase something.

00:20:54.690 --> 00:20:57.500
So as designers and
developers, we really

00:20:57.500 --> 00:21:02.930
want to try and limit the
amount of offline messages

00:21:02.930 --> 00:21:04.310
that we give out
to the end user,

00:21:04.310 --> 00:21:06.560
potentially even have
quite a bit of workflow

00:21:06.560 --> 00:21:10.590
available even while
that user is offline.

00:21:10.590 --> 00:21:13.258
So one thing you can do--

00:21:13.258 --> 00:21:15.300
and this is both a
performance benefit as well as

00:21:15.300 --> 00:21:16.380
an offline benefit--

00:21:16.380 --> 00:21:19.500
is cache certain parts of your
site locally into the browser.

00:21:19.500 --> 00:21:21.570
That allows you to go
through and allows the user

00:21:21.570 --> 00:21:23.430
to have some degree
of interactivity

00:21:23.430 --> 00:21:26.190
and continue to browse
inside of your web app

00:21:26.190 --> 00:21:28.830
without necessarily
having to be online.

00:21:28.830 --> 00:21:31.170
More importantly,
you can go through--

00:21:31.170 --> 00:21:35.670
and this is super useful in
workflow-based applications.

00:21:35.670 --> 00:21:37.770
So imagine if you're an airline.

00:21:37.770 --> 00:21:42.480
I don't necessarily need to be
online to go get my boarding

00:21:42.480 --> 00:21:45.340
pass or to go look up my trip
itinerary or know what gate--

00:21:45.340 --> 00:21:48.030
I guess I-- gates
change, but the idea here

00:21:48.030 --> 00:21:50.370
is that I can surface the
last known information

00:21:50.370 --> 00:21:54.577
about my flight without having
to necessarily be online.

00:21:54.577 --> 00:21:56.660
That's super useful for
traveling internationally,

00:21:56.660 --> 00:21:59.218
by the way.

00:21:59.218 --> 00:22:00.760
But certainly also,
you want to allow

00:22:00.760 --> 00:22:02.200
users to continue
to use the site

00:22:02.200 --> 00:22:03.770
and alert them when
they're offline.

00:22:03.770 --> 00:22:06.340
So pop open, and say, hey,
you're offline right now.

00:22:06.340 --> 00:22:08.800
This is an example from trivago.

00:22:08.800 --> 00:22:10.180
You're offline right now.

00:22:10.180 --> 00:22:13.780
I'm going to go and display what
I can, and you can reconnect.

00:22:13.780 --> 00:22:15.243
Or maybe you start
to go and start

00:22:15.243 --> 00:22:16.660
a workflow cache
in the background

00:22:16.660 --> 00:22:20.600
and resume it when the
user comes back online.

00:22:20.600 --> 00:22:23.960
And we know that in
addition to offline support,

00:22:23.960 --> 00:22:26.220
performance is
absolutely critical.

00:22:26.220 --> 00:22:28.790
There's a recent study
from Accenture and Google

00:22:28.790 --> 00:22:31.220
looking at users
in Asia-Pacific.

00:22:31.220 --> 00:22:33.260
53% of mobile visits
were likely to be

00:22:33.260 --> 00:22:36.410
abandoned if load times were
greater than three seconds.

00:22:36.410 --> 00:22:42.710
20% of drop in conversions were
found for every second of delay

00:22:42.710 --> 00:22:43.910
in a mobile website.

00:22:43.910 --> 00:22:49.010
So in other words, if you
don't have really fast sites,

00:22:49.010 --> 00:22:51.260
people won't actually go
through your experience,

00:22:51.260 --> 00:22:53.677
and they won't buy stuff.

00:22:53.677 --> 00:22:55.260
So we want to go
through and make sure

00:22:55.260 --> 00:22:57.573
that we use caching for
speed and reliability,

00:22:57.573 --> 00:22:59.490
speed up the load time
for returning visitors,

00:22:59.490 --> 00:23:02.310
ensure a consistent fast
experience, and also, again,

00:23:02.310 --> 00:23:05.080
lay that foundation
for offline experience.

00:23:05.080 --> 00:23:07.170
We also want to use
things like lazy loading.

00:23:07.170 --> 00:23:09.780
And lazy loading allows
you to go through and start

00:23:09.780 --> 00:23:11.730
to render the first
parts of the page

00:23:11.730 --> 00:23:14.880
but also work in the background
to go through and load things

00:23:14.880 --> 00:23:18.420
that don't necessarily need
to be up there and up in front

00:23:18.420 --> 00:23:21.790
of the user in order for that
first piece of interactivity.

00:23:21.790 --> 00:23:23.882
This is actually an
example from Tinder

00:23:23.882 --> 00:23:25.590
where they were going
through and looking

00:23:25.590 --> 00:23:27.132
at how their site
was built, and they

00:23:27.132 --> 00:23:29.670
found they were
loading a lot of images

00:23:29.670 --> 00:23:33.540
from places like Facebook
and other places and the user

00:23:33.540 --> 00:23:34.540
hadn't logged in yet.

00:23:34.540 --> 00:23:38.040
So what's the point of
pre-loading all this content

00:23:38.040 --> 00:23:40.500
if the user hasn't logged in
and hasn't created a Tinder

00:23:40.500 --> 00:23:41.000
account?

00:23:41.000 --> 00:23:44.638
So you can go through and say,
hey, login or create account,

00:23:44.638 --> 00:23:46.680
and be in the background
loading all these images

00:23:46.680 --> 00:23:48.720
so the user has
a fast experience

00:23:48.720 --> 00:23:51.580
once they've gotten
through that first gate.

00:23:51.580 --> 00:23:53.960
Performance budgets are
another way to do this.

00:23:53.960 --> 00:23:57.610
You can go through and set
milestones to make sure

00:23:57.610 --> 00:23:59.800
that technical debt doesn't
increase in your site

00:23:59.800 --> 00:24:01.930
and you don't go and
degrade the experience

00:24:01.930 --> 00:24:04.930
as new features get
implemented over time.

00:24:04.930 --> 00:24:07.570
Just some milestones that
you might think about--

00:24:07.570 --> 00:24:10.270
these are actually from web.dev,
and given the Accenture data,

00:24:10.270 --> 00:24:12.490
I might say three
seconds instead of five

00:24:12.490 --> 00:24:14.080
for time to interactive.

00:24:14.080 --> 00:24:17.560
You can also look at things
like the size of the code

00:24:17.560 --> 00:24:20.420
that you're having so
you can optimize that.

00:24:20.420 --> 00:24:23.920
But the milestones that you
really should be thinking about

00:24:23.920 --> 00:24:25.540
are First Contentful
Paint, which

00:24:25.540 --> 00:24:28.682
is basically when data starts to
be displayed into the browser.

00:24:28.682 --> 00:24:29.890
and then time to interactive.

00:24:29.890 --> 00:24:33.070
How quickly are loading that
site in order for the user

00:24:33.070 --> 00:24:35.080
to have a meaningful
interaction?

00:24:35.080 --> 00:24:37.420
Be using testing and
validation tools.

00:24:37.420 --> 00:24:40.240
We have things like
Lighthouse and web page

00:24:40.240 --> 00:24:42.850
calculate from Google that
can help you go through

00:24:42.850 --> 00:24:43.780
and optimize.

00:24:43.780 --> 00:24:45.130
But don't just optimize once.

00:24:45.130 --> 00:24:46.780
This isn't like
the rotisserie oven

00:24:46.780 --> 00:24:48.190
where you set it and forget it.

00:24:48.190 --> 00:24:50.770
You actually have to be doing
this as you go and re-release

00:24:50.770 --> 00:24:53.672
the site, or you can end up
with performance degradations.

00:24:53.672 --> 00:24:55.630
I'm going to skip that
slide because that's not

00:24:55.630 --> 00:24:58.580
a particular useful slide.

00:24:58.580 --> 00:25:01.960
So let's talk about this from
a case study perspective PWAs

00:25:01.960 --> 00:25:03.395
are great.

00:25:03.395 --> 00:25:05.020
They clearly offer
us a lot of benefit,

00:25:05.020 --> 00:25:07.450
but what does it look like
from an end-to-end experience?

00:25:07.450 --> 00:25:11.500
So this is actually
the trivago app again,

00:25:11.500 --> 00:25:14.658
just continuing with
our theme of travel.

00:25:14.658 --> 00:25:16.450
And you can see here,
when I go to trivago,

00:25:16.450 --> 00:25:18.025
I'm coming back as a user.

00:25:18.025 --> 00:25:21.040
It says, hey, would you
want us to go and add this

00:25:21.040 --> 00:25:21.890
to the home screen?

00:25:21.890 --> 00:25:24.940
So I can add trivago
out and come back to it.

00:25:24.940 --> 00:25:26.680
I type in San Francisco.

00:25:26.680 --> 00:25:29.110
I start to get my
wildly overpriced hotels

00:25:29.110 --> 00:25:32.290
here in the city because there's
Next and two other conferences

00:25:32.290 --> 00:25:34.540
going on right now.

00:25:34.540 --> 00:25:36.250
And all of a sudden,
I've experienced

00:25:36.250 --> 00:25:40.930
a connectivity disruption,
which you can see actually here

00:25:40.930 --> 00:25:42.400
in the middle pane.

00:25:42.400 --> 00:25:44.500
And so you can see,
even though I'm--

00:25:44.500 --> 00:25:45.990
sorry.

00:25:45.990 --> 00:25:47.052
Here in the middle pane.

00:25:47.052 --> 00:25:49.260
So as I go through and I go
through that connectivity

00:25:49.260 --> 00:25:50.850
disruption, I can
still go through

00:25:50.850 --> 00:25:52.050
and browse through
the site, look

00:25:52.050 --> 00:25:54.510
at some amount of data that's
been cached in the background.

00:25:54.510 --> 00:25:56.468
And then when I've reached
that limit of what's

00:25:56.468 --> 00:25:59.130
been cached onto the
device, I go through and get

00:25:59.130 --> 00:26:01.370
a notification,
hey, you're offline.

00:26:01.370 --> 00:26:05.265
Do you want to reconnect
and continue browsing?

00:26:05.265 --> 00:26:07.140
And obviously, when I
restore the connection,

00:26:07.140 --> 00:26:08.970
I can go out there
and buy my hotel.

00:26:08.970 --> 00:26:10.740
trivago's also done
a really good job

00:26:10.740 --> 00:26:12.480
of optimizing their
web experience

00:26:12.480 --> 00:26:14.170
for native and mobile.

00:26:14.170 --> 00:26:17.340
So you can see that I'm getting
a very similar look and feel,

00:26:17.340 --> 00:26:19.200
whether I'm using this
on my Android device

00:26:19.200 --> 00:26:21.430
or whether I'm using
this on my Chromebook.

00:26:21.430 --> 00:26:24.480
So they know and I
think they understand

00:26:24.480 --> 00:26:27.570
this idea of multi-modality
interaction and the fact

00:26:27.570 --> 00:26:29.700
that I'm likely going to
go to trivago once, look

00:26:29.700 --> 00:26:32.670
at some stuff, go to trivago
again, look at some stuff.

00:26:32.670 --> 00:26:34.382
And the devices that
I do that with are

00:26:34.382 --> 00:26:36.090
going to vary, so
let's make sure there's

00:26:36.090 --> 00:26:38.520
a consistent
experience across them.

00:26:38.520 --> 00:26:43.920
In the trivago case, they've
seen a ton of benefit

00:26:43.920 --> 00:26:46.480
in migrating a lot of their
functionality to the web.

00:26:46.480 --> 00:26:50.580
They've so far launched across
33 languages and 55 countries.

00:26:50.580 --> 00:26:54.600
But more importantly, they saw
a 97% increase in click-outs

00:26:54.600 --> 00:26:57.660
to hotel offers when they
actually went and optimized

00:26:57.660 --> 00:26:58.650
for the web experience.

00:26:58.650 --> 00:27:01.650
So rather than telling the user,
hey, go download something.

00:27:01.650 --> 00:27:03.630
Go through lots of
additional steps

00:27:03.630 --> 00:27:05.340
in order to get into
trivago, they're

00:27:05.340 --> 00:27:08.100
actually seeing people go
through a really elegant flow,

00:27:08.100 --> 00:27:10.830
and the users are then buying
at the end of that purchase.

00:27:10.830 --> 00:27:14.370
By going offline-- and while
the data set is relatively small

00:27:14.370 --> 00:27:15.450
in the case study--

00:27:15.450 --> 00:27:18.858
they still saw a 67%
improvement in return visits

00:27:18.858 --> 00:27:21.150
to the site when there's been
connectivity disruptions.

00:27:21.150 --> 00:27:24.210
So because they're providing a
really interactive experience

00:27:24.210 --> 00:27:26.030
even when the user's
offline, the user

00:27:26.030 --> 00:27:27.630
is willing to come
back or persist

00:27:27.630 --> 00:27:29.370
through the
connectivity disruption

00:27:29.370 --> 00:27:32.790
in order to go through and
engage with that brand.

00:27:35.370 --> 00:27:36.900
So we're set.

00:27:36.900 --> 00:27:39.800
We're all in for the web.

00:27:39.800 --> 00:27:42.350
Jon's actually going to go and
talk a little bit about some

00:27:42.350 --> 00:27:43.980
of the challenges we've
seen with customers,

00:27:43.980 --> 00:27:46.490
particularly as they think
about enterprise app workflows.

00:27:46.490 --> 00:27:48.500
And why don't I turn
the stage over to you

00:27:48.500 --> 00:27:50.410
to talk about native?

00:27:50.410 --> 00:27:51.202
JON MARKOFF: Hello?

00:27:51.202 --> 00:27:53.390
[INAUDIBLE] Thanks, Sean.

00:27:53.390 --> 00:27:56.520
So before we get started
here, when Sean first

00:27:56.520 --> 00:27:58.230
asked me to do
this talk with him,

00:27:58.230 --> 00:28:01.800
the first thought in my mind
was, this is a horrible idea.

00:28:01.800 --> 00:28:04.500
Web versus native
almost never goes well.

00:28:04.500 --> 00:28:06.283
There is always a
Twitter war or something

00:28:06.283 --> 00:28:07.200
that comes up from it.

00:28:07.200 --> 00:28:09.400
So I apologize in advance.

00:28:09.400 --> 00:28:12.040
I'm going to be as
objective as possible.

00:28:12.040 --> 00:28:14.040
A lot of people ask my
advice, and again, that's

00:28:14.040 --> 00:28:16.470
why we're doing this is
because people ask us so often,

00:28:16.470 --> 00:28:17.700
what do we build for?

00:28:17.700 --> 00:28:21.000
And obviously, the decision to
build for mobile or web really

00:28:21.000 --> 00:28:23.400
depends on what you're
doing, and I never just say,

00:28:23.400 --> 00:28:25.790
oh, just because
I work on Android,

00:28:25.790 --> 00:28:27.540
you should always build
for Android or you

00:28:27.540 --> 00:28:28.920
should always build for native.

00:28:28.920 --> 00:28:30.003
So I would never say that.

00:28:30.003 --> 00:28:33.700
So just want to preamble
this section of the talk.

00:28:33.700 --> 00:28:35.640
So with that said, my
name is Jon Markoff.

00:28:35.640 --> 00:28:39.180
I lead developer relations
for Android Enterprise.

00:28:39.180 --> 00:28:41.830
And, as Sean mentioned, the
web has a lot of benefits

00:28:41.830 --> 00:28:43.080
and has a lot of great things.

00:28:43.080 --> 00:28:45.720
If you're building native apps
in especially the enterprise

00:28:45.720 --> 00:28:47.640
space, there's a
few other things

00:28:47.640 --> 00:28:50.110
you might want to
take into account.

00:28:50.110 --> 00:28:54.660
So if your app is going to store
a lot of information locally,

00:28:54.660 --> 00:28:56.340
web browsers,
especially in mobile,

00:28:56.340 --> 00:28:59.735
have more restrictions on
how much storage and data

00:28:59.735 --> 00:29:00.610
you can save offline.

00:29:00.610 --> 00:29:03.840
Chrome, for example,
you can save up

00:29:03.840 --> 00:29:05.665
to 6% of the free
space on the device

00:29:05.665 --> 00:29:07.290
whereas something
like Safari, you only

00:29:07.290 --> 00:29:08.340
get 50 megabytes total.

00:29:08.340 --> 00:29:12.460
So if your app is for
workers in a warehouse

00:29:12.460 --> 00:29:15.817
or delivering packages or
something like that, where

00:29:15.817 --> 00:29:17.900
you're not going to have
connectivity all the time

00:29:17.900 --> 00:29:20.040
and you need to download a lot
of different things, especially

00:29:20.040 --> 00:29:22.498
if you are a service provider
or someone at a cable company

00:29:22.498 --> 00:29:23.450
fixing--

00:29:23.450 --> 00:29:26.370
and you need videos to be able
to understand and see different

00:29:26.370 --> 00:29:28.755
situations where you're--

00:29:28.755 --> 00:29:30.880
you just need to have a
lot of space on the device.

00:29:30.880 --> 00:29:32.505
So if you're downloading
videos, you're

00:29:32.505 --> 00:29:34.470
downloading a lot of
local content for offline,

00:29:34.470 --> 00:29:36.580
the web might be a bit
challenging there for you,

00:29:36.580 --> 00:29:39.693
especially if your device
is lower-powered and doesn't

00:29:39.693 --> 00:29:41.110
really come with
a lot of storage.

00:29:41.110 --> 00:29:44.180
So if you only have
four gigs of free space,

00:29:44.180 --> 00:29:46.470
you're going to
have not too much

00:29:46.470 --> 00:29:49.920
with even Chrome in this case.

00:29:49.920 --> 00:29:52.520
So as Sean mentioned,
the web does give you

00:29:52.520 --> 00:29:54.970
a lot of [INAUDIBLE] hardware.

00:29:54.970 --> 00:29:57.270
But there's a few things
that are different.

00:29:57.270 --> 00:30:02.960
So, for example,
NFC and attestation

00:30:02.960 --> 00:30:05.630
are two really large ones
that are missing from the web.

00:30:05.630 --> 00:30:07.550
You do have the ability
to use biometrics

00:30:07.550 --> 00:30:09.840
so you can authenticate
on the web.

00:30:09.840 --> 00:30:14.030
But if you want to do more
hardware-backed Android

00:30:14.030 --> 00:30:18.560
Keystore or crypto, you'll
need to have a native app

00:30:18.560 --> 00:30:19.940
to access those features.

00:30:22.660 --> 00:30:24.520
And again, this is
just an overview

00:30:24.520 --> 00:30:27.970
of all the different web
browsers and the features

00:30:27.970 --> 00:30:28.720
that they support.

00:30:33.530 --> 00:30:37.880
So let's talk about
native apps security.

00:30:37.880 --> 00:30:40.550
If you store files in
the local file system,

00:30:40.550 --> 00:30:42.350
who can access them?

00:30:42.350 --> 00:30:46.553
Ideally, only your user and
the person using the device.

00:30:46.553 --> 00:30:48.220
But what happens if
the device is stolen

00:30:48.220 --> 00:30:50.053
or if the device is
compromised in some way?

00:30:50.053 --> 00:30:52.610
How do you ensure that your data
is actually stored properly,

00:30:52.610 --> 00:30:54.602
especially in offline mode?

00:30:54.602 --> 00:30:56.560
With the web, you don't
really have any options

00:30:56.560 --> 00:30:58.660
of how to change this
data because unless you

00:30:58.660 --> 00:31:01.780
do some sort of like JavaScript
crypto, which would not really

00:31:01.780 --> 00:31:07.120
be able to take advantage of the
Trusted Execution Environment

00:31:07.120 --> 00:31:09.250
on Android.

00:31:09.250 --> 00:31:13.690
So for data at rest-- so if you
want to actually store files

00:31:13.690 --> 00:31:16.460
and you want to encrypt
them on the device--

00:31:16.460 --> 00:31:18.580
you can use the Android
Keystore with a native app

00:31:18.580 --> 00:31:21.910
to ensure that your
keys are never leaked

00:31:21.910 --> 00:31:23.650
and will never come
off the device.

00:31:23.650 --> 00:31:25.900
Your application cannot even
get the key material once

00:31:25.900 --> 00:31:26.550
it's created.

00:31:29.840 --> 00:31:32.420
As a vendor at Oreo, we added
sensitive data protection.

00:31:32.420 --> 00:31:35.720
What this means
is, as a user, you

00:31:35.720 --> 00:31:38.330
must be using a phone
that's been unlocked.

00:31:38.330 --> 00:31:40.770
So the key won't be
available to the application

00:31:40.770 --> 00:31:43.770
if the device isn't unlocked.

00:31:43.770 --> 00:31:46.220
And to add to this
even more, they also

00:31:46.220 --> 00:31:48.080
support biometric
authentication.

00:31:48.080 --> 00:31:50.330
So when you're creating keys
with the Android Keystore

00:31:50.330 --> 00:31:52.880
you can actually set flags
to say, always require

00:31:52.880 --> 00:31:53.990
authentication.

00:31:53.990 --> 00:31:55.767
Have a timeout on
the authentication

00:31:55.767 --> 00:31:57.350
and require sensitive
data protection.

00:31:57.350 --> 00:32:00.560
So you could actually
have it require the user

00:32:00.560 --> 00:32:03.410
to be present for any time
the key's even accessed,

00:32:03.410 --> 00:32:05.240
so depending on
the level of data

00:32:05.240 --> 00:32:08.280
that you're working on here.

00:32:08.280 --> 00:32:10.350
With key attestation,
you can also

00:32:10.350 --> 00:32:12.870
ensure that your keys
haven't been compromised

00:32:12.870 --> 00:32:16.695
and that they never
left the device.

00:32:16.695 --> 00:32:17.820
What about data in transit?

00:32:17.820 --> 00:32:19.140
What if I'm sending data?

00:32:19.140 --> 00:32:21.070
What if I'm calling
really sensitive services,

00:32:21.070 --> 00:32:24.090
and I need to make sure
that someone hasn't

00:32:24.090 --> 00:32:26.400
injected a bad certificate?

00:32:26.400 --> 00:32:29.530
You can use OCSP, which is
the Online Certificate Status

00:32:29.530 --> 00:32:32.630
Protocol, which is built
into Android as of Nougat,

00:32:32.630 --> 00:32:34.410
which is Android 7.

00:32:34.410 --> 00:32:39.620
And that allows you to add
checks to actually ensure

00:32:39.620 --> 00:32:42.140
that you can talk to your
certificate authority

00:32:42.140 --> 00:32:45.140
and make sure that the
certificate is valid

00:32:45.140 --> 00:32:47.948
and it is what it says it is.

00:32:47.948 --> 00:32:50.240
One other thing you can do
as well is TLD verification.

00:32:50.240 --> 00:32:52.220
So that's the top-level
domains and making sure

00:32:52.220 --> 00:32:55.610
that someone hasn't created
a fake one or someone hasn't

00:32:55.610 --> 00:32:58.890
tried to compromise the device.

00:32:58.890 --> 00:33:01.030
Protected Confirmation.

00:33:01.030 --> 00:33:03.397
We launched the Titan
Keys on Pixel devices,

00:33:03.397 --> 00:33:05.230
and we just announced
that we're taking that

00:33:05.230 --> 00:33:09.940
to more devices as of yesterday
with Cloud and on Android.

00:33:09.940 --> 00:33:11.980
And Protected
Confirmation allows

00:33:11.980 --> 00:33:15.520
you to verify that
the transaction you're

00:33:15.520 --> 00:33:18.280
trying to accomplish
is being executed

00:33:18.280 --> 00:33:19.930
by a actual person
on the device.

00:33:19.930 --> 00:33:21.730
So similar to a
YubiKey, if you use

00:33:21.730 --> 00:33:25.472
one to log in if you
have a corporate laptop,

00:33:25.472 --> 00:33:29.270
it basically make sure that
you are actually present,

00:33:29.270 --> 00:33:33.170
so someone's not emulating or
stimulating your application

00:33:33.170 --> 00:33:36.090
in Android Studio
or other tools.

00:33:36.090 --> 00:33:37.840
And it provides the
fact that you're there

00:33:37.840 --> 00:33:40.660
because if someone packet
sniffs your services,

00:33:40.660 --> 00:33:43.002
they can try to make
calls and replay calls.

00:33:43.002 --> 00:33:44.710
But with built-in
hardware in the device,

00:33:44.710 --> 00:33:45.543
you can't fake that.

00:33:48.400 --> 00:33:50.470
SafetyNet.

00:33:50.470 --> 00:33:52.090
One other thing that
you can't enforce

00:33:52.090 --> 00:33:55.060
when you're building just
a pure web app on mobile

00:33:55.060 --> 00:33:58.060
is, how do I know if my
device has been rooted?

00:33:58.060 --> 00:34:02.580
How do I know if I'm actually
on a real Android device at all?

00:34:02.580 --> 00:34:06.113
Because you can rip
APKs from the web.

00:34:06.113 --> 00:34:08.280
You can rip them off your
phone and try to run them.

00:34:08.280 --> 00:34:11.092
You can deconstruct them
and figure out, how can

00:34:11.092 --> 00:34:12.300
I try to compromise this app?

00:34:12.300 --> 00:34:15.750
How can I try to replay and run
this app without doing that?

00:34:15.750 --> 00:34:19.860
SafetyNet allows you to
validate that you are actually

00:34:19.860 --> 00:34:22.469
on an actual device and
it's a CTS-compliant device.

00:34:22.469 --> 00:34:25.469
That means that
Google knows about it,

00:34:25.469 --> 00:34:27.179
and it's a device
that meets the test

00:34:27.179 --> 00:34:28.471
specifications of what we have.

00:34:28.471 --> 00:34:30.900
So someone just didn't
build a random open source

00:34:30.900 --> 00:34:33.880
version of Android just to try
to compromise and run your app.

00:34:37.440 --> 00:34:39.900
As an example,
here's what SafetyNet

00:34:39.900 --> 00:34:41.520
looks like after you call it.

00:34:41.520 --> 00:34:43.228
I'm not going to jump
into the code here,

00:34:43.228 --> 00:34:45.780
but just as an example,
the two last fields

00:34:45.780 --> 00:34:48.030
in this JSON signature
[INAUDIBLE] that come back

00:34:48.030 --> 00:34:50.070
are basicIntegrity,
which means has

00:34:50.070 --> 00:34:52.782
this device been rooted at
all, and ctsProfileMatch,

00:34:52.782 --> 00:34:54.449
which means that it's
actually a device.

00:34:58.010 --> 00:35:00.740
And in more detail,
SafetyNet has

00:35:00.740 --> 00:35:07.550
a few steps that it takes, and
this is to ensure that one,

00:35:07.550 --> 00:35:09.050
you know that the
call is happening.

00:35:09.050 --> 00:35:12.800
You know what user's is
actually calling the service,

00:35:12.800 --> 00:35:15.470
and then ensuring from
Google that I actually have

00:35:15.470 --> 00:35:18.440
a real response from SafetyNet.

00:35:18.440 --> 00:35:21.133
So to jump through
these really quick,

00:35:21.133 --> 00:35:22.550
the first thing
you're going to do

00:35:22.550 --> 00:35:24.300
is you're going to
generate a nonce, which

00:35:24.300 --> 00:35:26.480
is a one-time-use
token, and that

00:35:26.480 --> 00:35:29.270
should be tied to
whatever user is logged

00:35:29.270 --> 00:35:31.125
in through your application.

00:35:31.125 --> 00:35:32.750
And this is something
you save and make

00:35:32.750 --> 00:35:34.552
sure on your back-end
yourself that you

00:35:34.552 --> 00:35:36.260
know was only used
once and you know it's

00:35:36.260 --> 00:35:38.690
tied to the correct person.

00:35:38.690 --> 00:35:44.750
In Android, you'd call the API
to attest that this device has

00:35:44.750 --> 00:35:48.060
not been rooted and to reach
out to Google to say, hey,

00:35:48.060 --> 00:35:49.310
do you know about this device?

00:35:49.310 --> 00:35:50.102
Has it been rooted?

00:35:50.102 --> 00:35:51.800
What's going on here?

00:35:51.800 --> 00:35:54.050
You're going to get back the
JSON web signature that I

00:35:54.050 --> 00:35:58.483
showed you earlier that
has a key signature and all

00:35:58.483 --> 00:36:00.650
the different fields that
you might want to look at,

00:36:00.650 --> 00:36:04.430
timestamps, APK, is
this compromised or not?

00:36:04.430 --> 00:36:07.640
And you need to send
this back to your server

00:36:07.640 --> 00:36:09.780
and check all these fields
just to make sure, hey,

00:36:09.780 --> 00:36:11.330
does this make sense?

00:36:11.330 --> 00:36:12.090
I just sent this.

00:36:12.090 --> 00:36:13.430
Does the time makes sense?

00:36:13.430 --> 00:36:14.713
Does the signature make sense?

00:36:14.713 --> 00:36:16.130
And you reach out
to Google to ask

00:36:16.130 --> 00:36:20.900
one last time, hey, by the
way, is this a real response?

00:36:20.900 --> 00:36:22.970
Does the signature match?

00:36:22.970 --> 00:36:24.440
If yes, that's great.

00:36:24.440 --> 00:36:24.960
You're fine.

00:36:24.960 --> 00:36:27.170
If not, then you know that
you can disable that user

00:36:27.170 --> 00:36:28.320
on your back-end as well.

00:36:28.320 --> 00:36:30.695
So that means that you know
that this user has been using

00:36:30.695 --> 00:36:32.810
a compromised device,
and you can block them

00:36:32.810 --> 00:36:35.570
from accessing any
services after that.

00:36:38.410 --> 00:36:40.180
One other thing as well that--

00:36:40.180 --> 00:36:45.010
you have some device management
with Chrome and web browsers

00:36:45.010 --> 00:36:47.500
on mobile, but you
have much more control

00:36:47.500 --> 00:36:50.110
over this with third-party
tools like EMMs

00:36:50.110 --> 00:36:53.720
which allow you to inject
settings into your application.

00:36:53.720 --> 00:36:55.900
So if you're
building for the web,

00:36:55.900 --> 00:36:58.220
you would have to build in
all the different features.

00:36:58.220 --> 00:37:01.870
So if I wanted to have a
configurable application where

00:37:01.870 --> 00:37:05.363
I have different settings
based on different user types,

00:37:05.363 --> 00:37:07.780
you actually have to build
that into your web application.

00:37:07.780 --> 00:37:12.250
Or what you can do is, if you're
using like a commercial EMM,

00:37:12.250 --> 00:37:16.030
you can inject settings
via managed config, which

00:37:16.030 --> 00:37:18.692
will configure each user's
device from an admin console.

00:37:18.692 --> 00:37:20.650
So you don't actually
have to write custom code

00:37:20.650 --> 00:37:22.108
to handle the
different situations,

00:37:22.108 --> 00:37:24.100
if that makes sense.

00:37:24.100 --> 00:37:24.895
Android.

00:37:24.895 --> 00:37:26.020
Not sure if everyone knows.

00:37:26.020 --> 00:37:28.760
There is a concept
of a work profile.

00:37:28.760 --> 00:37:33.160
So if your enterprise
or company supports

00:37:33.160 --> 00:37:34.630
bringing your own
device to work,

00:37:34.630 --> 00:37:36.340
you can install a
work profile, which

00:37:36.340 --> 00:37:38.320
is a separate container
on the device that

00:37:38.320 --> 00:37:43.600
allows you to basically separate
your work and personal life.

00:37:43.600 --> 00:37:46.912
It maintains privacy and it
will actually allows you to now

00:37:46.912 --> 00:37:48.370
you can turn it
off at night if you

00:37:48.370 --> 00:37:50.590
want to to not be getting
pings and work emails

00:37:50.590 --> 00:37:52.427
and everything all the time.

00:37:52.427 --> 00:37:54.760
And then on the corporate
side, if your company actually

00:37:54.760 --> 00:37:57.640
hands you a phone or
device, this device

00:37:57.640 --> 00:38:00.430
has a lot more features
that you can pull logs.

00:38:00.430 --> 00:38:02.203
There's deep inspection.

00:38:02.203 --> 00:38:04.120
And it has full admin
visibility on everything

00:38:04.120 --> 00:38:05.380
that's going on on it.

00:38:05.380 --> 00:38:08.660
So in these kinds of cases,
a lot more enterprises that

00:38:08.660 --> 00:38:09.760
are using--

00:38:09.760 --> 00:38:14.200
they use both, but for some
of the really specific use

00:38:14.200 --> 00:38:18.110
cases we're talking about with
especially custom hardware,

00:38:18.110 --> 00:38:20.440
you probably have a
company-owned device.

00:38:20.440 --> 00:38:22.000
So here's some
information on-- we

00:38:22.000 --> 00:38:23.417
don't go through
all these things,

00:38:23.417 --> 00:38:25.090
but if you're building
for work profile,

00:38:25.090 --> 00:38:29.470
your application automatically
has to handle a few situations

00:38:29.470 --> 00:38:31.040
and scenarios.

00:38:31.040 --> 00:38:34.300
And this is important
because it allows

00:38:34.300 --> 00:38:37.588
you to run your app in a limited
privileged container, which

00:38:37.588 --> 00:38:39.130
it might not
necessarily be aware of.

00:38:39.130 --> 00:38:41.107
So a few things here.

00:38:41.107 --> 00:38:42.940
Not really going to go
over in super detail,

00:38:42.940 --> 00:38:45.970
but your app might not
act like it usually does.

00:38:45.970 --> 00:38:48.220
There's going to be features
like the camera might not

00:38:48.220 --> 00:38:48.580
exist.

00:38:48.580 --> 00:38:50.163
You might not be
able to share things.

00:38:50.163 --> 00:38:53.775
You might not be able to save
files like you normally do.

00:38:53.775 --> 00:38:55.900
And you can't handle
notifications in the same way.

00:38:55.900 --> 00:38:57.317
They work a little
bit differently

00:38:57.317 --> 00:39:00.250
in the work profile.

00:39:00.250 --> 00:39:02.290
So I mentioned Managed
Configurations earlier.

00:39:02.290 --> 00:39:05.947
Just to go through what
that flow looks like,

00:39:05.947 --> 00:39:07.780
when you're building
an Android application,

00:39:07.780 --> 00:39:11.060
you're going to define
like how it's configurable.

00:39:11.060 --> 00:39:13.330
So let's say I'm a email client.

00:39:13.330 --> 00:39:16.090
Well, you're probably going to
have an email address, server,

00:39:16.090 --> 00:39:17.620
and depending on
that server type,

00:39:17.620 --> 00:39:20.450
you might have port and a
bunch of other information.

00:39:20.450 --> 00:39:22.630
So you'd generate a little
XML file, which defines,

00:39:22.630 --> 00:39:24.355
hey, I'm expecting an email
address, which is a string,

00:39:24.355 --> 00:39:26.320
and I'm expecting a
port, which is an end,

00:39:26.320 --> 00:39:30.290
and maybe know an address,
which is a string as well.

00:39:30.290 --> 00:39:32.980
And what that does is Play
automatically knows and reads

00:39:32.980 --> 00:39:35.380
that.

00:39:35.380 --> 00:39:38.440
So that gets injected
into Google Play, which

00:39:38.440 --> 00:39:42.188
talks to an EMM which says,
OK, this app has these settings

00:39:42.188 --> 00:39:42.730
to configure.

00:39:42.730 --> 00:39:45.100
So when they're adding
from the EMM perspective

00:39:45.100 --> 00:39:48.100
or your admin is adding
a new phone or device,

00:39:48.100 --> 00:39:50.383
they would be able to set
those fields automatically.

00:39:50.383 --> 00:39:52.300
So you just start your
phone up and, oh, look.

00:39:52.300 --> 00:39:53.410
I know this is Jon.

00:39:53.410 --> 00:39:54.140
This is his email address.

00:39:54.140 --> 00:39:54.682
This is this.

00:39:54.682 --> 00:39:56.980
So I open the app and
it just works already.

00:39:56.980 --> 00:39:58.870
I don't have to go try to figure
out what my email settings are,

00:39:58.870 --> 00:39:59.380
for example.

00:40:03.440 --> 00:40:07.780
Yeah, so that's the
native side of things.

00:40:07.780 --> 00:40:09.400
We do have more resources.

00:40:09.400 --> 00:40:12.880
A couple things I didn't mention
from a design perspective,

00:40:12.880 --> 00:40:16.150
and I mentioned that there's
all the different browsers

00:40:16.150 --> 00:40:17.025
and challenges there.

00:40:17.025 --> 00:40:19.525
Obviously, also, there's a lot
of different Android devices,

00:40:19.525 --> 00:40:21.100
a lot of different
Android versions.

00:40:21.100 --> 00:40:22.815
We launched Jetpack
two years ago

00:40:22.815 --> 00:40:26.440
at Google I/O, which is
a more holistic support

00:40:26.440 --> 00:40:27.610
library for Android.

00:40:27.610 --> 00:40:29.860
And that allows you
to more easily handle

00:40:29.860 --> 00:40:32.176
backwards compatibility.

00:40:32.176 --> 00:40:33.080
Yeah.

00:40:33.080 --> 00:40:33.980
So I think that's it.

00:40:33.980 --> 00:40:34.752
I think we can--

00:40:34.752 --> 00:40:36.460
I'll pass it back to
Sean, and then we'll

00:40:36.460 --> 00:40:37.540
open it up for questions.

00:40:37.540 --> 00:40:38.832
SEAN GINEVAN: Yeah, absolutely.

00:40:38.832 --> 00:40:40.720
So thanks, Jon.

00:40:40.720 --> 00:40:42.220
I'm going to steal
the clicker back.

00:40:42.220 --> 00:40:44.030
JON MARKOFF: Yep.

00:40:44.030 --> 00:40:46.870
SEAN GINEVAN: So hopefully this
gives you a high-level overview

00:40:46.870 --> 00:40:49.720
of building for enterprise.

00:40:49.720 --> 00:40:52.060
I think what we often
coach folks on--

00:40:52.060 --> 00:40:55.090
and Jon brought this up at the
beginning of the presentation--

00:40:55.090 --> 00:40:57.513
this is not a religious war.

00:40:57.513 --> 00:40:58.930
At the end of the
day, what you're

00:40:58.930 --> 00:41:01.450
building for needs to
depend on the projects

00:41:01.450 --> 00:41:04.250
that you're actually building
inside of your organization.

00:41:04.250 --> 00:41:06.670
There are lots of cases
where building for the web

00:41:06.670 --> 00:41:09.880
is a really simple way
to get basic workflows

00:41:09.880 --> 00:41:11.590
into the hands of your users.

00:41:11.590 --> 00:41:14.500
It's certainly, from a
brand and a B2C perspective,

00:41:14.500 --> 00:41:18.670
an absolutely critical way to
be engaging with your customers.

00:41:18.670 --> 00:41:21.430
But it's not necessarily
the right technology,

00:41:21.430 --> 00:41:23.260
depending on what
types of workflows

00:41:23.260 --> 00:41:26.120
you're building out
for your enterprise.

00:41:26.120 --> 00:41:28.137
So as Jon mentioned,
we have a ton of tools

00:41:28.137 --> 00:41:30.220
out there, whether you're
trying to understand how

00:41:30.220 --> 00:41:31.900
to build out enterprise apps.

00:41:31.900 --> 00:41:34.133
Use some of the tools
that Jon had mentioned.

00:41:34.133 --> 00:41:35.800
Definitely encourage
you to go to those.

